#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523015 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos(2t-\\frac{2\\pi}{3})+8,\\,y=4\\sin(2t-\\frac{2\\pi}{3})-9,\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le t<\\frac{5\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-8\\sin(2t-\\frac{2\\pi}{3}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8\\cos(2t-\\frac{2\\pi}{3})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n8\\cos(2t-\\frac{2\\pi}{3})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{7\\pi}{12}, \\frac{13\\pi}{12}, \\frac{19\\pi}{12}, \\frac{25\\pi}{12}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{12}\\right) & =-8\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{13\\pi}{12}\\right) & =8\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{19\\pi}{12}\\right) & =-8\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{25\\pi}{12}\\right) & =8\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $$\n\\displaystyle t=\\frac{7\\pi}{12}, \\frac{13\\pi}{12}, \\frac{19\\pi}{12}, \\frac{25\\pi}{12}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523022 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\pi t)-8t,\\,y={e}^{1t},\\,0<t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is horizontal when the tangent line to the curve is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{t}-8,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=1{e}^{1t}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{t}-8=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)=1{e}^{1\\times (1/8)}=e^{\\frac{1}{8}}\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=\\frac{1}{8}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523025 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\cos(2t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4})-4,\\,y=3\\sin(2t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4})-7,\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le t<\\frac{5\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-12\\sin(2t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\cos(2t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6\\cos(2t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{5\\pi}{8}, \\frac{9\\pi}{8}, \\frac{13\\pi}{8}, \\frac{17\\pi}{8}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{8}\\right) & =-12\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{9\\pi}{8}\\right) & =12\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{13\\pi}{8}\\right) & =-12\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{17\\pi}{8}\\right) & =12\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $$\n\\displaystyle t=\\frac{5\\pi}{8}, \\frac{9\\pi}{8}, \\frac{13\\pi}{8}, \\frac{17\\pi}{8}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523026 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7(t-4),\\,y=3{t}^{3}-18t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=7,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=9{t}^{2}-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n9({t}^{2}-2)=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(-\\sqrt{2}) & =7\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\sqrt{2}) & =7\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523029 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})+3,\\,y=3\\sin(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})+4,\\,2\\pi\\le t<4\\pi.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-8\\sin(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{3}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{29\\pi}{12}, \\frac{35\\pi}{12}, \\frac{41\\pi}{12}, \\frac{47\\pi}{12}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{29\\pi}{12}\\right) & =-8\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{35\\pi}{12}\\right) & =8\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{41\\pi}{12}\\right) & =-8\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{47\\pi}{12}\\right) & =8\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $$\n\\displaystyle t=\\frac{29\\pi}{12}, \\frac{35\\pi}{12}, \\frac{41\\pi}{12}, \\frac{47\\pi}{12}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523064 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos(6t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})+6,\\,y=6\\sin(6t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})-1,\\,\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\le t<\\frac{4\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-24\\sin(6t-\\frac{\\pi}{6}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=36\\cos(6t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n36\\cos(6t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{7\\pi}{9}, \\frac{17\\pi}{18}, \\frac{10\\pi}{9}, \\frac{23\\pi}{18}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{9}\\right) & =-24\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{17\\pi}{18}\\right) & =24\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{10\\pi}{9}\\right) & =-24\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{23\\pi}{18}\\right) & =24\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $$\n\\displaystyle t=\\frac{7\\pi}{9}, \\frac{17\\pi}{18}, \\frac{10\\pi}{9}, \\frac{23\\pi}{18}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523073 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+8,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})-8,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the normal to the curve is vertical at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) & =-5\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right) & =5\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523079 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})-10,\\,y=1\\sin(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})+0,\\,\\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\le t<\\frac{7\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-4\\sin(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n2\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{11\\pi}{6}, \\frac{7\\pi}{3}, \\frac{17\\pi}{6}, \\frac{10\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{11\\pi}{6}\\right) & =4\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{3}\\right) & =-4\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{17\\pi}{6}\\right) & =4\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{10\\pi}{3}\\right) & =-4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $$\n\\displaystyle t=\\frac{11\\pi}{6}, \\frac{7\\pi}{3}, \\frac{17\\pi}{6}, \\frac{10\\pi}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523083 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+11,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the normal to the curve is vertical at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) & =-6\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right) & =6\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523089 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{5t},\\,y=4{t}^{2}-8t,\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is vertical when the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=5{e}^{5t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8t-8\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n8t-8=0\\,⟹\\,t=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(1)=5{e}^{5}=5 e^{5}\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523202 :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(1,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{ }i+(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(4\\cdot (1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))+4\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\text{ }i+(2(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =8 \\sin{(1 )}\\text{ }i-2 \\cos{(1 )}\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $-8 \\sin{(1 )}\\,i+2 \\cos{(1 )}\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523211 :question/problem "In what unit direction does the function $f(x,y,z)=\\ln (x^{3}y^{2}z^{4})$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,1,1)$?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln (x^{3}y^{2}z^{4})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\ln (x^{3}y^{2}z^{4})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\ln (x^{3}y^{2}z^{4}))⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{1}{x^{3}y^{2}z^{4}}\\cdot 3x^{2}y^{2}z^{4},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{3}y^{2}z^{4}}\\cdot 2x^{3}y^{1}z^{4},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{3}y^{2}z^{4}}\\cdot 4x^{3}y^{2}z^{3}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{3}{x},\\,\\frac{2}{y},\\,\\frac{4}{z}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(1,1,1) & =⟨\\frac{3}{1},\\,\\frac{2}{1},\\,\\frac{4}{1}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨3,\\,2,\\,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding unit direction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{∇f(1,1,1)}{‖∇f(1,1,1)‖} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(4)^{2}}}⟨3,\\,2,\\,4⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{3 \\sqrt{29}}{29},\\,\\frac{2 \\sqrt{29}}{29},\\,\\frac{4 \\sqrt{29}}{29}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the unit direction of $$\n\\displaystyle ⟨\\frac{3 \\sqrt{29}}{29},\\,\\frac{2 \\sqrt{29}}{29},\\,\\frac{4 \\sqrt{29}}{29}⟩.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523221 :question/problem "In what unit direction does the function $f(x,y,z)=\\ln (x^{2}y^{1}z^{6})$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,1,3)$?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln (x^{2}y^{1}z^{6})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\ln (x^{2}y^{1}z^{6})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\ln (x^{2}y^{1}z^{6}))⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{1}{x^{2}y^{1}z^{6}}\\cdot 2x^{1}y^{1}z^{6},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{2}y^{1}z^{6}}\\cdot x^{2}y^{0}z^{6},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{2}y^{1}z^{6}}\\cdot 6x^{2}y^{1}z^{5}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{2}{x},\\,\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{6}{z}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(1,1,3) & =⟨\\frac{2}{1},\\,\\frac{1}{1},\\,\\frac{6}{3}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨2,\\,1,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding unit direction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{∇f(1,1,3)}{‖∇f(1,1,3)‖} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(2)^{2}}}⟨2,\\,1,\\,2⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the unit direction of $$\n\\displaystyle ⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523222 :question/problem "In what unit direction does the function $f(x,y,z)=\\ln (x^{6}y^{4}z^{4})$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(2,2,2)$?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y,z)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln (x^{6}y^{4}z^{4})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\ln (x^{6}y^{4}z^{4})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\ln (x^{6}y^{4}z^{4}))⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{1}{x^{6}y^{4}z^{4}}\\cdot 6x^{5}y^{4}z^{4},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{6}y^{4}z^{4}}\\cdot 4x^{6}y^{3}z^{4},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{6}y^{4}z^{4}}\\cdot 4x^{6}y^{4}z^{3}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{6}{x},\\,\\frac{4}{y},\\,\\frac{4}{z}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(2,2,2) & =⟨\\frac{6}{2},\\,\\frac{4}{2},\\,\\frac{4}{2}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨3,\\,2,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding unit direction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =-\\frac{∇f(2,2,2)}{‖∇f(2,2,2)‖} \\\\ & =\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(2)^{2}}}⟨3,\\,2,\\,2⟩ \\\\ & =⟨-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17}}{17},\\,-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17},\\,-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the unit direction of $$\n\\displaystyle ⟨-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17}}{17},\\,-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17},\\,-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17}⟩.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523223 :question/problem "In what unit direction does the function $f(x,y,z)=\\ln (x^{4}y^{1}z^{3})$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(3,3,3)$?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y,z)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(3,3,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln (x^{4}y^{1}z^{3})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\ln (x^{4}y^{1}z^{3})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\ln (x^{4}y^{1}z^{3}))⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{1}{x^{4}y^{1}z^{3}}\\cdot 4x^{3}y^{1}z^{3},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{4}y^{1}z^{3}}\\cdot x^{4}y^{0}z^{3},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{4}y^{1}z^{3}}\\cdot 3x^{4}y^{1}z^{2}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{4}{x},\\,\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{3}{z}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(3,3,3) & =⟨\\frac{4}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{3}{3}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{4}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding unit direction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =-\\frac{∇f(3,3,3)}{‖∇f(3,3,3)‖} \\\\ & =\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)^{2}+\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{2}+(1)^{2}}}⟨\\frac{4}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,1⟩ \\\\ & =⟨-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{26}}{13},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{26},\\,-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{26}}{26}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the unit direction of $$\n\\displaystyle ⟨-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{26}}{13},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{26},\\,-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{26}}{26}⟩.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523226 :question/problem "In what unit direction does the function $f(x,y,z)=\\ln (x^{5}y^{2}z^{2})$ increase most rapidly at the point $(3,2,2)$?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(3,2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln (x^{5}y^{2}z^{2})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\ln (x^{5}y^{2}z^{2})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\ln (x^{5}y^{2}z^{2}))⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{1}{x^{5}y^{2}z^{2}}\\cdot 5x^{4}y^{2}z^{2},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{5}y^{2}z^{2}}\\cdot 2x^{5}y^{1}z^{2},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{5}y^{2}z^{2}}\\cdot 2x^{5}y^{2}z^{1}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{5}{x},\\,\\frac{2}{y},\\,\\frac{2}{z}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(3,2,2) & =⟨\\frac{5}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{2},\\,\\frac{2}{2}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{5}{3},\\,1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding unit direction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{∇f(3,2,2)}{‖∇f(3,2,2)‖} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(1)^{2}}}⟨\\frac{5}{3},\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{5 \\sqrt{43}}{43},\\,\\frac{3 \\sqrt{43}}{43},\\,\\frac{3 \\sqrt{43}}{43}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the unit direction of $$\n\\displaystyle ⟨\\frac{5 \\sqrt{43}}{43},\\,\\frac{3 \\sqrt{43}}{43},\\,\\frac{3 \\sqrt{43}}{43}⟩.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524019 :question/problem "What is the slope $m$ of the line $\\overset{↔}{AB}?$\n\n$m\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "As we found previously, the points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,3)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(4,1),$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, we compute the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-3}{4-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524114 :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\ln{\\left(4 x + 5 y \\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\ln{\\left(4 x + 5 y \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{4}{4 x + 5 y} \\\\ f_{y}(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\ln{\\left(4 x + 5 y \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{5}{4 x + 5 y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y)&=f_{x}(x,y)\\,i+f_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{4 x + 5 y}\\,i+\\frac{5}{4 x + 5 y}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524116 :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sin{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\sin{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=z \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)} \\\\ f_{y}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\sin{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=4 \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)} \\\\ f_{z}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial z}\\left(\\sin{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=x \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y,z)&=f_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+f_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+f_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\ &= z \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\,i+4 \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\,j+x \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524124 :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\ln{\\left(2 x + 6 y \\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\ln{\\left(2 x + 6 y \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{2}{2 x + 6 y} \\\\ f_{y}(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\ln{\\left(2 x + 6 y \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{6}{2 x + 6 y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y)&=f_{x}(x,y)\\,i+f_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{2 x + 6 y}\\,i+\\frac{6}{2 x + 6 y}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524155 :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =4 x + 3 y,\\quad ∇f(x,y)=(4)\\,i+(3)\\,j \\\\ g(x,y) & =4 x - 2 y,\\quad ∇g(x,y)=(4)\\,i+(-2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=g∇f+f∇g.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇g(x,y) \\\\  & =(4 x - 2 y)((4)\\,i+(3)\\,j)+(4 x + 3 y)((4)\\,i+(-2)\\,j) \\\\  & =(4 x - 2 y)(4)i+(4 x - 2 y)(3)j+(4 x + 3 y)(4)i+(4 x + 3 y)(-2)j \\\\  & =(16 x - 8 y)i+(12 x - 6 y)j+(16 x + 12 y)i+(- 8 x - 6 y)j \\\\  & =(32 x + 4 y)\\,i+(4 x - 12 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524157 :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =2 x - 2 y,\\quad ∇f(x,y)=(2)\\,i+(-2)\\,j \\\\ g(x,y) & =- 3 x - y,\\quad ∇g(x,y)=(-3)\\,i+(-1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=g∇f+f∇g.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇g(x,y) \\\\  & =(- 3 x - y)((2)\\,i+(-2)\\,j)+(2 x - 2 y)((-3)\\,i+(-1)\\,j) \\\\  & =(- 3 x - y)(2)i+(- 3 x - y)(-2)j+(2 x - 2 y)(-3)i+(2 x - 2 y)(-1)j \\\\  & =(- 6 x - 2 y)i+(6 x + 2 y)j+(- 6 x + 6 y)i+(- 2 x + 2 y)j \\\\  & =(- 12 x + 4 y)\\,i+(4 x + 4 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524174 :question/problem "What is the gradient of the product $f(x,y)h(x,y)$ if $f(x,y)=(x-3){e}^{(y+4)}$ and $h(x,y)=(y+4){e}^{(x-3)}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fh]=f\\,∇\\,h+h\\,∇\\,f\\,\\,\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-3){e}^{(y+4)})={e}^{(y+4)} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-3){e}^{(y+4)})=(x-3){e}^{(y+4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }j={e}^{(y+4)}\\text{ }i+(x-3){e}^{(y+4)}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the partial derivatives of $h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((y+4){e}^{(x-3)})=(y+4){e}^{(x-3)} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y+4){e}^{(x-3)})={e}^{(x-3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y) & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\text{ }j=(y+4){e}^{(x-3)}\\text{ }i+{e}^{(x-3)}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the product rule, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)h(x,y)] & =(x-3){e}^{(y+4)}((y+4){e}^{(x-3)}\\text{ }i+{e}^{(x-3)}\\text{ }j)+(y+4){e}^{(x-3)}({e}^{(y+4)}\\text{ }i+(x-3){e}^{(y+4)}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =(x-3)(y+4){e}^{(x-3)+(y+4)}\\text{ }i+(x-3){e}^{(x-3)+(y+4)}\\text{ }j+(y+4){e}^{(x-3)+(y+4)}\\text{ }i+(x-3)(y+4){e}^{(x-3)+(y+4)}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =((x-3)+1)(y+4){e}^{(x-3)+(y+4)}\\text{ }i+(x-3)(1+(y+4)){e}^{(x-3)+(y+4)}\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524177 :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y,z)g(x,y,z),$ given that $f(x,y,z)=x^{2} y^{3} z - x y z^{3}$ and $g(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{x y z}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y,z)$ and $g(x,y,z)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=g∇f+f∇g.\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} y^{3} z - x y z^{3})=2 x y^{3} z - y z^{3} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} y^{3} z - x y z^{3})=3 x^{2} y^{2} z - x z^{3} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x^{2} y^{3} z - x y z^{3})=x^{2} y^{3} - 3 x y z^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}k=(2 x y^{3} z - y z^{3})\\,i+(3 x^{2} y^{2} z - x z^{3})\\,j+(x^{2} y^{3} - 3 x y z^{2})\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the partial derivatives of $g(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{1}{x y z}\\right)=- \\frac{1}{x^{2} y z} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{1}{x y z}\\right)=- \\frac{1}{x y^{2} z} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}\\left(\\frac{1}{x y z}\\right)=- \\frac{1}{x y z^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}i+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}j+\\frac{∂g}{∂z}k=\\left(- \\frac{1}{x^{2} y z}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{1}{x y^{2} z}\\right)\\,j+\\left(- \\frac{1}{x y z^{2}}\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y,z)g(x,y,z)] & =g(x,y,z)∇f(x,y,z)+f(x,y,z)∇g(x,y,z) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{x y z}\\right)((2 x y^{3} z - y z^{3})\\,i+(3 x^{2} y^{2} z - x z^{3})\\,j+(x^{2} y^{3} - 3 x y z^{2})\\,k)+(x^{2} y^{3} z - x y z^{3})(\\left(- \\frac{1}{x^{2} y z}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{1}{x y^{2} z}\\right)\\,j+\\left(- \\frac{1}{x y z^{2}}\\right)\\,k) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{x y z}\\right)(2 x y^{3} z - y z^{3})i+\\left(\\frac{1}{x y z}\\right)(3 x^{2} y^{2} z - x z^{3})j+\\left(\\frac{1}{x y z}\\right)(x^{2} y^{3} - 3 x y z^{2})k+(x^{2} y^{3} z - x y z^{3})\\left(- \\frac{1}{x^{2} y z}\\right)i+(x^{2} y^{3} z - x y z^{3})\\left(- \\frac{1}{x y^{2} z}\\right)j+(x^{2} y^{3} z - x y z^{3})\\left(- \\frac{1}{x y z^{2}}\\right)k \\\\  & =(2 y^{2} - \\frac{z^{2}}{x})i+(3 x y - \\frac{z^{2}}{y})j+(\\frac{x y^{2}}{z} - 3 z)k+(- y^{2} + \\frac{z^{2}}{x})i+(- x y + \\frac{z^{2}}{y})j+(- \\frac{x y^{2}}{z} + z)k \\\\  & =(y^{2})\\,i+(2 x y)\\,j+(- 2 z)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524179 :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =- 2 x + 2 y,\\quad ∇f(x,y)=(-2)\\,i+(2)\\,j \\\\ g(x,y) & =- x - y,\\quad ∇g(x,y)=(-1)\\,i+(-1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=g∇f+f∇g.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇g(x,y) \\\\  & =(- x - y)((-2)\\,i+(2)\\,j)+(- 2 x + 2 y)((-1)\\,i+(-1)\\,j) \\\\  & =(- x - y)(-2)i+(- x - y)(2)j+(- 2 x + 2 y)(-1)i+(- 2 x + 2 y)(-1)j \\\\  & =(2 x + 2 y)i+(- 2 x - 2 y)j+(2 x - 2 y)i+(2 x - 2 y)j \\\\  & =(4 x)\\,i+(- 4 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524183 :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =- x + y,\\quad ∇f(x,y)=(-1)\\,i+(1)\\,j \\\\ g(x,y) & =3 x - 4 y,\\quad ∇g(x,y)=(3)\\,i+(-4)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=g∇f+f∇g.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇g(x,y) \\\\  & =(3 x - 4 y)((-1)\\,i+(1)\\,j)+(- x + y)((3)\\,i+(-4)\\,j) \\\\  & =(3 x - 4 y)(-1)i+(3 x - 4 y)(1)j+(- x + y)(3)i+(- x + y)(-4)j \\\\  & =(- 3 x + 4 y)i+(3 x - 4 y)j+(- 3 x + 3 y)i+(4 x - 4 y)j \\\\  & =(- 6 x + 7 y)\\,i+(7 x - 8 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524187 :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =4 x - 2 y,\\quad ∇f(x,y)=(4)\\,i+(-2)\\,j \\\\ g(x,y) & =- 3 x - 3 y,\\quad ∇g(x,y)=(-3)\\,i+(-3)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=g∇f+f∇g.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇g(x,y) \\\\  & =(- 3 x - 3 y)((4)\\,i+(-2)\\,j)+(4 x - 2 y)((-3)\\,i+(-3)\\,j) \\\\  & =(- 3 x - 3 y)(4)i+(- 3 x - 3 y)(-2)j+(4 x - 2 y)(-3)i+(4 x - 2 y)(-3)j \\\\  & =(- 12 x - 12 y)i+(6 x + 6 y)j+(- 12 x + 6 y)i+(- 12 x + 6 y)j \\\\  & =(- 24 x - 6 y)\\,i+(- 6 x + 12 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524193 :question/problem "What is the gradient of the product $f(x,y)h(x,y)$ if $f(x,y)=(x+1){e}^{(y+4)}$ and $h(x,y)=(y+4){e}^{(x+1)}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fh]=f\\,∇\\,h+h\\,∇\\,f\\,\\,\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+1){e}^{(y+4)})={e}^{(y+4)} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x+1){e}^{(y+4)})=(x+1){e}^{(y+4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }j={e}^{(y+4)}\\text{ }i+(x+1){e}^{(y+4)}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the partial derivatives of $h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((y+4){e}^{(x+1)})=(y+4){e}^{(x+1)} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y+4){e}^{(x+1)})={e}^{(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y) & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\text{ }j=(y+4){e}^{(x+1)}\\text{ }i+{e}^{(x+1)}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the product rule, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)h(x,y)] & =(x+1){e}^{(y+4)}((y+4){e}^{(x+1)}\\text{ }i+{e}^{(x+1)}\\text{ }j)+(y+4){e}^{(x+1)}({e}^{(y+4)}\\text{ }i+(x+1){e}^{(y+4)}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =(x+1)(y+4){e}^{(x+1)+(y+4)}\\text{ }i+(x+1){e}^{(x+1)+(y+4)}\\text{ }j+(y+4){e}^{(x+1)+(y+4)}\\text{ }i+(x+1)(y+4){e}^{(x+1)+(y+4)}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =((x+1)+1)(y+4){e}^{(x+1)+(y+4)}\\text{ }i+(x+1)(1+(y+4)){e}^{(x+1)+(y+4)}\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524276 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(2,1,2)$, $B(3,4,2)$, $C(5,4,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(3i+4j+2k)-(2i+j+2k) \\\\  & =i+3j \\\\  & =⟨1,3,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(5i+4j+2k)-(2i+j+2k) \\\\  & =3i+3j \\\\  & =⟨3,3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 0)i-(1\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-6k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{6}{2}=3.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{10} \\\\ 6 & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{10} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524280 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(-1,-1,0)$, $B(-3,2,0)$, $C(-2,2,0).$\n\n$CH=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-3i+2j)-(-i-j) \\\\  & =-2i+3j \\\\  & =⟨-2,3,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-2i+2j)-(-i-j) \\\\  & =-i+3j \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 0)i-((-2)\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 0)j+((-2)\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-3k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{13} \\\\ 3 & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{13} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524285 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(-1,0,0)$, $B(-3,1,0)$, $C(1,2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-3i+j)-(-i) \\\\  & =-2i+j \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(i+2j)-(-i) \\\\  & =2i+2j \\\\  & =⟨2,2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 0)i-((-2)\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 0)j+((-2)\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =-6k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{6}{2}=3.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 6 & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524293 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(1,1,1)$, $B(-1,4,1)$, $C(2,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-i+4j+k)-(i+j+k) \\\\  & =-2i+3j \\\\  & =⟨-2,3,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i+k)-(i+j+k) \\\\  & =i-j \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 0)i-((-2)\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 0)j+((-2)\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{13} \\\\ 1 & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{13} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524299 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(0,-2,-1)$, $B(1,0,-1)$, $C(2,-4,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(i-k)-(-2j-k) \\\\  & =i+2j \\\\  & =⟨1,2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i-4j-2k)-(-2j-k) \\\\  & =2i-2j-k \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 0)i-(1\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =-2i+j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{41} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{41}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{41} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{205}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524306 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(0,0,2)$, $B(2,-3,2)$, $C(2,2,3).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i-3j+2k)-2k \\\\  & =2i-3j \\\\  & =⟨2,-3,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i+2j+3k)-2k \\\\  & =2i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-3)\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 0)i-(2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 0)j+(2\\cdot 2-2\\cdot (-3))k \\\\  & =-3i-2j+10k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-2,10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{10}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{113} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{113}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{113}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{113}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{113}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{13} \\\\ \\sqrt{113} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{13} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{113}}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1469}}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524307 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(1,-2,-1)$, $B(2,1,-1)$, $C(4,0,2).$\n\n$CH=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i+j-k)-(i-2j-k) \\\\  & =i+3j \\\\  & =⟨1,3,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(4i+2k)-(i-2j-k) \\\\  & =3i+2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨3,2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 0)i-(1\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =9i-3j-7k \\\\  & =⟨9,-3,-7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{9}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-7)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{139} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{139}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{139}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{139}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{139}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{10} \\\\ \\sqrt{139} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{10} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{139}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1390}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524309 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(1,-2,1)$, $B(2,1,1)$, $C(4,0,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i+j+k)-(i-2j+k) \\\\  & =i+3j \\\\  & =⟨1,3,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(4i-k)-(i-2j+k) \\\\  & =3i+2j-2k \\\\  & =⟨3,2,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 0)i-(1\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-6i+2j-7k \\\\  & =⟨-6,2,-7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-7)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{89} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{89}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{89}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{89}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{89}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{10} \\\\ \\sqrt{89} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{10} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{89}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{890}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524312 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(-1,-1,0)$, $B(-3,2,0)$, $C(-2,2,3).$\n\n$CH=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-3i+2j)-(-i-j) \\\\  & =-2i+3j \\\\  & =⟨-2,3,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-2i+2j+3k)-(-i-j) \\\\  & =-i+3j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 0)i-((-2)\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 0)j+((-2)\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =9i+6j-3k \\\\  & =⟨9,6,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{9}^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{126} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{13} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{14} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{13} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{182}}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524316 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(1,-2,-1)$, $B(2,1,-1)$, $C(4,0,-1).$\n\n$CH=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i+j-k)-(i-2j-k) \\\\  & =i+3j \\\\  & =⟨1,3,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(4i-k)-(i-2j-k) \\\\  & =3i+2j \\\\  & =⟨3,2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 0)i-(1\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-7k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-7)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{7}{2}=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\frac{7}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{10} \\\\ 7 & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{10} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{10}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524983 :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+2x}$ at the point where $x=-1$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=3{x}^{2}+2x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x+2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (6x+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{6x+2}{2\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-1} & ={\\left(\\frac{6x+2}{2\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+2x}}\\right)|}_{x=-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1)+2}{2\\sqrt{3(-1)^{2}+2(-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{2\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525021 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=(3{x}^{2}-10)^{4},$ find the slope at the point $(2,16).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{4},\\,g=3{x}^{2}-10\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4{g}^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=6x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =4{g}^{3}\\cdot (6x) \\\\  & =24x(3{x}^{2}-10)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & ={24x{(3{x}^{2}-10)}^{3}|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =24(2){(3(2)^{2}-10)}^{3} \\\\  & =384.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
